==Albanian==
{{wikipedia|Mali (gjeografi)|lang=sq}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l-lite|sq|mall}}
* {{l-lite|sq|malj}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh-lite|sq|sqj-pro|sc=Latinx|*mala}},<ref>{{R:sq:Orel|page=243 → ''(“Proto-Albanian *mala was borrowed into Rum. mal ('bank')”)''}}</ref> from {{der-lite|sq|xil|*mol-on}}. [[Wikipedia|Vladimir Orel]] proposed {{cog-lite|lt|mala|malà|land}} and {{cog-lite|lv|mala||bank, shore}} as cognates. Proto-Albanian {{m|sqj-pro|*mal-}} reflecting an ancient Balkan toponym.<ref>{{cite book |title=Etymologisches Wörterbuch der albanesischen Sprache |author=Gustav Meyer |year=1891 |isbn= |publisher=Trübner |page=273}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |title=Albanesische Studien III. Lautlehre des indogermanischen Bestandteile des Albanesischen |author=Gustav Meyer |year=1892 |isbn= |publisher=Carl Gerold's Sohn |page=63, 78}}</ref> Preserved in patroynms, ethnonym {{m-lite|sq|malësor||highlander, mountaineer}}, in toponym (historical and ethnographic region) [[Malësia]] (north Albania and Montenegro). In Kosovo ([[Wikipedia|Malishevë, Gjilan]], [[Wikipedia|Mališevo]], Prizren), in Serbia ([[Wikipedia|Maleševo (Golubac)]], [[Wikipedia|Maleševo (Rekovac)]]) and the name of [[Wikipedia|Maleshevo Mountain]] (North Macedonia and Bulgaria).<ref>{{cite book |title=Namenforschung 1. Teilband (Name Studies Volume 1, Les noms propres Tome 1)  |author=Ernst Eichler, Gerold Hilty, Heinrich Löffler, Hugo Steger, Ladislav Zgusta |year=1995 |isbn=97831102034243110203421 |publisher=Walter de Gruyter & Co., Berlin - New York |page=718 → ''(Chapter: 104. Illyrian-Albanian Toponyms)''[https://books.google.ch/books?id=ck1CLzJq9vAC&pg=PA718&dq=mal+albanian+illyrian&hl=de&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwifmsyatdvZAhULvhQKHf1EB5sQ6AEIDDAA#v=onepage&q=mal%20albanian%20illyrian&f=false]}}</ref> Gil'Ferding proposed {{cog-lite|sa|sc=Deva|मरु||tr=marú|wilderness, mountain, rock}} as a cognate.
According to [[wikipedia|Michel Morvan]] a common [[pre-Indo-European]] {{der-lite|sq|qfa-sub}} with {{cog-lite|eu|malda||slope}} and {{m-lite|eu|malkor||precipice}}<ref>{{cite book |title=Les origines linguistiques du Basque (The linguistic origins of Basque) |author=Michel Morvan |publisher=Presses Universitaires de Bordeaux |year=1996 |isbn=2867811821}}</ref> (cf. pre-Indo-European [[geonymic]] root *mal (*mel, *mol), {{cog-lite|ta|sc=Taml|மலை||tr=malai|hill, mountain}} and {{cog-lite|ml|sc=Mlym|മല||tr=mala|id}}).

La Piana and Huld suggested {{cog-lite|ang|sc=Latinx|molda||forehead}} and {{cog-lite|sa|sc=Deva|मूर्धन्||tr=mūrdhan|head, top, summit}}, both derived from {{cog-lite|ine-pro|sc=Latinx|*ml̥Hdʰṓ}}. Also connected to {{cog-lite|grc|sc=polytonic|*μλωθρός|tr=*mlōthrós}}, {{m-lite|grc|sc=polytonic|μέλαθρον||tr=mélathron|ridgepole}}, {{m-lite|grc|sc=polytonic|βλαστάνω||tr=blastánō|to sprout, grow}}. Compare also {{cog-lite|grc|sc=polytonic|βλωθρός||tr=blōthrós|lofty}}, {{cog-lite|ae|sc=Avst|𐬐𐬀𐬨𐬆𐬭𐬆𐬜𐬋|𐬐𐬀-𐬨𐬆𐬭𐬆𐬜𐬋|tr=ka-mər<sup><small>ə</small></sup>δō|demon's head}}, with a semantic development from ‘head’ > ‘summit’, compare {{m-lite|sq|malë||tongue tip, tree top}}) > ‘mountain’.

Alternatively, from {{der-lite|sq|xdc|-}} or a Paleo-Balkan {{der-lite|sq|qfa-sub}}, cognate to {{cog-lite|ro|mal}}.<ref>KatKatičić, R. Ancient Languages of the Balkans. 1975. p. 152</ref>

